(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-musculature-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Axial Musculature — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Into what three layers can the intrinsic (deep) back muscles be subdivided?","choices":["Cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spinal regions only","Anterior, middle, and posterior muscular layers","Flexor, extensor, and rotator functional layers","Superficial intrinsic, intermediate intrinsic, and deep intrinsic"],"answer":3,"exp":"The intrinsic back muscles can be further subdivided into superficial intrinsic, intermediate intrinsic, and deep intrinsic layers, which work to flex/stabilize/extend the spine laterally and cannot be differentiated clinically. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which muscle group makes up the superficial layer of extrinsic back muscles?","choices":["Splenius capitis and splenius cervicis of the neck","The erector spinae muscle group by itself","Trapezius, latissimus dorsi, levator scapulae, and rhomboid muscles","Serratus posterior superior and inferior, deep to the rhomboids"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superficial layer of extrinsic back muscles consists of the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, levator scapulae, and rhomboid muscles — appendicular muscles that position the pectoral girdle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which intermediate extrinsic back muscle elevates the ribs for ventilation?","choices":["Serratus posterior inferior","Serratus posterior superior","Trapezius","Latissimus dorsi"],"answer":1,"exp":"Serratus posterior superior elevates the ribs for ventilation, innervated by thoracic nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which three muscles make up the erector spinae group, an example of the intermediate intrinsic back muscles?","choices":["Longus capitis, longus colli, and quadratus lumborum","Multifidus, rotatores, and interspinales muscles","Splenius capitis, splenius cervicis, and semispinalis","Spinalis thoracis, longissimus thoracis, and iliocostalis lumborum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The erector spinae group is made up of three muscles: spinalis thoracis (most medial), longissimus thoracis, and iliocostalis lumborum (most lateral). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which erector spinae muscle is the most lateral of the three, running from the iliac crest/sacral crest/lumbar spinous processes to the inferior aspect of ribs 6-12?","choices":["Iliocostalis lumborum","Longissimus thoracis","Multifidus muscle","Spinalis thoracis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Iliocostalis lumborum is the most lateral of the three erector spinae muscles, running from the iliac crest, sacral crest, and lumbar spinous processes to the inferior aspect of ribs 6-12 near their angles, producing extension and rib depression. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which deep intrinsic back muscle extends the vertebral column and rotates the body toward the opposite side of the muscle?","choices":["Interspinales","Intertransversarii","Splenius capitis","Multifidus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Multifidus extends the vertebral column and rotates the body toward the side opposite the muscle, innervated by cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spinal nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which deep intrinsic back muscle produces lateral flexion of the vertebral column, distinct from the extension-focused actions of nearby deep muscles?","choices":["Rotatores muscle","Intertransversarii","Interspinales muscle","Multifidus muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Intertransversarii produces lateral flexion of the vertebral column. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which two muscles, when acting together, flex the neck, and when acting alone, rotate the neck to that side?","choices":["Longus capitis and longus colli","Splenius capitis and splenius cervicis","Multifidus and rotatores","Quadratus lumborum and iliocostalis lumborum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Longus capitis and longus colli, positioned on either side of the neck, together flex the neck, while acting alone, each rotates the neck to that side. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes the vertebral column laterally and is innervated by thoracic and lumbar spinal nerves?","choices":["Erector spinae group","Longus colli muscle","Quadratus lumborum","Longus capitis muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Quadratus lumborum flexes the vertebral column laterally, innervated by thoracic and lumbar spinal nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What do all three scalene muscles (anterior, middle, posterior) do?","choices":["Depress the ribs for exhalation","Flex the vertebral column laterally only","Rotate the head to the opposite side","Elevate the ribs for inhalation"],"answer":3,"exp":"All scalene muscles (anterior, middle, and posterior) elevate the ribs, assisting with inhalation. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the action of the external intercostal muscles, and where do their fibers run?","choices":["Depress the ribs; same fiber direction as external intercostals","Compress the abdomen; unrelated to rib position","Elevate the ribs; from the inferior border of each rib to the superior border of the rib below","Depress the ribs; from the superior border of each rib to the inferior border of the rib above"],"answer":2,"exp":"The external intercostal muscles elevate the ribs, with fibers running from the inferior border of each rib to the superior border of the rib below. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the action of the internal intercostal muscles?","choices":["Compresses the abdomen","Depresses the ribs","Flexes the vertebral column","Elevates the ribs"],"answer":1,"exp":"The internal intercostal muscles depress the ribs, with fibers running from the superior border of each rib to the inferior border of the rib above. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is the action of the transversus thoracis muscle?","choices":["Compresses the abdomen","Elevates the ribs","Depresses the ribs","Extends the vertebral column"],"answer":2,"exp":"Transversus thoracis depresses the ribs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How do the superior and inferior serratus posterior muscles differ in their action on the ribs?","choices":["Superior depresses the ribs during exhalation; inferior elevates the ribs during exhalation","Superior elevates the ribs for ventilation; inferior depresses the ribs for ventilation","Neither muscle affects rib position; both instead stabilize the scapula against the rib cage","Both muscles elevate the ribs equally during every phase of breathing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Serratus posterior superior elevates the ribs for ventilation, while serratus posterior inferior depresses the ribs for ventilation, both innervated by thoracic nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which three abdominal wall muscles compress the abdomen, with two of them also depressing the ribs and laterally flexing the torso?","choices":["Rectus abdominis, external oblique, and diaphragm","External oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis","Internal oblique, diaphragm, and levator ani","Quadratus lumborum, rectus abdominis, and transversus abdominis"],"answer":1,"exp":"External oblique and internal oblique both compress the abdomen, depress the ribs, and laterally flex the torso, while transversus abdominis compresses the abdomen. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the specific action of the transversus abdominis, distinguishing it from the oblique muscles?","choices":["Elevates the ribs during forced inhalation, unlike any of the abdominal wall muscles","Extends the vertebral column, an action shared with the erector spinae group","Compresses the abdomen only, without the rib-depression or lateral-flexion actions of the obliques","Compresses the abdomen and also depresses the ribs, acting identically to the external and internal obliques"],"answer":2,"exp":"Transversus abdominis compresses the abdomen, without the additional rib-depression and lateral-torso-flexion actions listed for the external and internal obliques. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What are the origin and insertion of the rectus abdominis?","choices":["Origin: ribs 5 through 7 only; insertion: the pubic tubercle, without reaching the xiphoid process","Origin: the iliac crest of the pelvis; insertion: the inferior borders of ribs 6 through 12 near their angles","Origin: superior surface of the pubis around the symphysis; insertion: inferior surfaces of ribs 5-7 and the xiphoid process","Origin: the xiphoid process of the sternum; insertion: directly onto the pubic symphysis itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rectus abdominis originates on the superior surface of the pubis around the symphysis and inserts on the inferior surfaces of ribs 5-7 and the xiphoid process; it depresses the ribs and flexes the vertebral column. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What two structures make up the midline and segmented appearance of the rectus abdominis?","choices":["The linea alba and tendinous inscriptions","The costal cartilages and xiphoid process","The rectus sheath and inguinal ligament","The linea alba and the diaphragm's central tendon"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectus abdominis consists of the linea alba (the midline fibrous band) and tendinous inscriptions (which create its segmented appearance). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What happens to thoracic cavity volume when the diaphragm contracts, and what respiratory phase does this correspond to?","choices":["The diaphragm lowers, decreasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to exhalation","The diaphragm lowers, increasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to inhalation","The diaphragm rises, increasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to exhalation","The diaphragm rises, decreasing thoracic volume; this corresponds to inhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"When the diaphragm contracts, it lowers, increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity, which corresponds to inhalation. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What nerve innervates the diaphragm?","choices":["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Intercostal nerves","Phrenic nerves (C3-C5)","Thoracic spinal nerves only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The diaphragm is innervated by the phrenic nerves, arising from spinal levels C3-C5. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the origins of the diaphragm, converging on the central tendinous sheet?","choices":["Xiphoid process, ribs 7-12 and associated costal cartilages, and anterior surfaces of the lumbar spine","Ribs 1 through 6 along with the manubrium and body of the sternum anteriorly","The iliac crest of the pelvis together with the anterior surface of the sacrum","Only the xiphoid process of the sternum, with no rib or vertebral contribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragm originates from the xiphoid process, ribs 7-12 and their associated costal cartilages, and the anterior surfaces of the lumbar spine, converging on the central tendinous sheet. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the main functions of the muscles of the perineum and pelvic diaphragm?","choices":["Support pelvic organs, flex the joints of the sacrum/coccyx, and control movement of material through the urethra and anus","Rotate the head from side to side, flex the cervical spine, and stabilize the neck during swallowing","Extend the vertebral column, help maintain upright standing posture, and stabilize the trunk during lifting","Elevate the ribs to assist forced inhalation during heavy exercise, exertion, or singing"],"answer":0,"exp":"The main functions of the muscles of the perineum and pelvic diaphragm are to support the organs of the pelvic cavity, flex the joints of the sacrum and coccyx, and control the movement of material through the urethra and anus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What spinal nerve levels innervate the pelvic floor muscles, and what is recommended for evaluating dysfunction?","choices":["T1-T5; perform a physical exam assessing facial movement and symmetry","S2-S4; image the spine, since the individual muscles cannot be differentiated clinically","L1-L3; assess hip flexion strength against manual muscle resistance","C3-C5; assess phrenic nerve function via diaphragmatic excursion on imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pelvic floor muscles are innervated by S2-S4; since dysfunction of any individual muscle cannot be differentiated clinically, imaging the spine is recommended, with referral to GI, OB/GYN, or urology as appropriate. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Into what two triangles is the perineal region (pelvic floor) divided?","choices":["Medial triangle and lateral triangle divisions","Urogenital triangle (anterior) and anal triangle (posterior)","Superficial triangle and deep triangle regions only","Superior triangle and inferior triangle divisions"],"answer":1,"exp":"The perineal region is divided into two triangles: the urogenital triangle (anterior triangle) and the anal triangle (posterior triangle), separated by the superficial transverse perineal muscle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What structure separates the urogenital triangle from the anal triangle?","choices":["The levator ani muscle of the pelvic floor","The entire pelvic diaphragm structure","The superficial transverse perineal muscle","The external anal sphincter muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superficial transverse perineal muscle separates the urogenital triangle from the anal triangle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which muscles make up the superficial layer of the urogenital triangle?","choices":["Superficial transverse perineal, ischiocavernosus, and bulbospongiosus","Deep transverse perineal and the external urethral sphincter","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles of the anal triangle","Obturator internus and the external anal sphincter"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superficial urogenital triangle muscles are the superficial transverse perineal, ischiocavernosus, and bulbospongiosus. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which muscles make up the deep layer of the urogenital triangle?","choices":["Levator ani and iliococcygeus of the pelvic diaphragm","Bulbospongiosus and coccygeus, spanning both triangles","Deep transverse perineal and external urethral sphincter","Superficial transverse perineal and ischiocavernosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deep urogenital triangle muscles are the deep transverse perineal and the external urethral sphincter. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which muscle group forms the pelvic diaphragm portion of the anal triangle, including the iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus?","choices":["Levator ani","Obturator internus","External anal sphincter","Coccygeus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Levator ani, which includes the iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus, is a key muscle of the anal triangle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Besides levator ani, which two additional muscles are listed as part of the anal triangle?","choices":["Obturator internus (lateral) and coccygeus, along with the external anal sphincter","Deep transverse perineal and the external urethral sphincter muscles","Ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus of the urogenital triangle","Superficial transverse perineal muscle and the pelvic diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Besides levator ani, the anal triangle includes the (lateral) obturator internus, coccygeus, and the external anal sphincter. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Why can't dysfunction of an individual intrinsic (deep) back muscle typically be differentiated clinically from the others in its layer?","choices":["Because these deep back muscles are anatomically situated well outside the range that can be reached and isolated during a standard physical examination of the back","Because the superficial, intermediate, and deep intrinsic muscles all work together to flex/stabilize/extend the spine laterally, making isolated dysfunction difficult to pinpoint on exam","Because only a single intrinsic back muscle actually exists in the entire back, leaving no other muscle from which to distinguish its dysfunction","Because these deep muscles receive no sensory or proprioceptive innervation whatsoever from the spinal nerves that supply the back"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superficial, intermediate, and deep intrinsic back muscles work together to flex, stabilize, and extend the spine laterally, so dysfunction of one individual muscle generally cannot be differentiated clinically from the others. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"}]};
